<h2>Upfront cost and long-term value</h2>
<p>Asphalt shingles usually have the lower installed price. Metal roofs cost more because of the material itself, the trim and flashing details, and the specialized installation. Standing seam systems, with concealed fasteners and raised seams, are typically the most expensive residential metal option, while exposed-fastener panels cost less but have screws that need periodic attention.</p>
<p>The value question is about time. If you plan to stay in your home for many years, a metal roof's longer expected service life may offset part of its higher price. If you expect to move in a few years, the extra investment may not come back to you in the same way. Exact pricing depends on the roof's size, pitch and details, so the only reliable way to compare is to get written estimates for both on your actual roof.</p>

<h2>Weather performance in Friendswood</h2>
<h3>Wind and storms</h3>
<p>Wind is a major consideration this close to the Gulf Coast. Both materials are sold with wind ratings, and the rating only holds when the product is installed exactly as the manufacturer specifies, including fastener type, placement and edge details. Dimensional (architectural) shingles are generally rated for higher winds than basic three-tab shingles. Metal panel systems can be engineered for high wind uplift resistance. Ask any contractor which rating applies to the specific product and installation method they are proposing.</p>
<p>If your home is in the Galveston County part of Friendswood and you have windstorm coverage through the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association, the roofing product and installation may need to meet specific requirements and a windstorm inspection. Confirm with your insurer or agent before choosing materials.</p>
<h3>Hail</h3>
<p>Hail can bruise or crack standard shingles and knock off granules. Impact-resistant shingles are designed to handle hail better, and some insurers offer premium discounts for qualifying products. Metal roofing resists punctures well but can dent, and dents may be considered cosmetic under some insurance policies. Ask your insurer how each material would be treated before deciding.</p>
<h3>Heat and humidity</h3>
<p>Long summers are hard on roofing. Asphalt shingles slowly lose oils and granules under sun and heat, which is part of why attic ventilation matters so much. Metal roofs with light-colored or reflective coatings can reflect more sunlight than dark shingles, which may reduce heat transferred into the attic. In practice, attic ventilation and insulation have a big influence on comfort and energy use with either material.</p>
<p>Humidity encourages algae streaks on shingles. Many shingles now include algae-resistant granules. Metal roofs are less prone to visible streaking, though debris and moisture should still be kept from collecting.</p>

<h2>Noise, appearance and home style</h2>
<p>A common concern is that metal roofs are loud in the rain. On a house with solid decking, underlayment and an insulated attic, most homeowners find the difference modest. Metal over open framing, as on a barn, is noticeably louder.</p>
<p>Appearance is personal. Shingles offer a classic look and a wide range of colors and textures that blend with most neighborhoods. Metal can look modern or farmhouse-style and is sometimes used as an accent on porches or dormers alongside a shingle main roof. If you live in a neighborhood with a homeowners association, check its rules on roofing materials and colors before you decide.</p>

<h2>Maintenance and repairs</h2>
<p>Shingle roofs are easy to repair: individual shingles can usually be replaced, and parts are widely available. Over time you may need to replace pipe boots, reseal flashing and address wind-lifted shingles.</p>
<p>Metal roofs generally need less routine attention, but exposed-fastener systems have screws with rubber washers that can loosen or degrade and should be checked periodically. Repairs to metal require matching panels, trim and coatings, and someone experienced with metal details. Both materials benefit from clean gutters and valleys and a regular check, as described in our <a href="/blog/friendswood-roof-maintenance-checklist/">seasonal maintenance checklist</a>.</p>

<h2>Which is right for your home?</h2>
<p>Asphalt shingles may suit you best if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Keeping upfront cost down is a priority</li>
  <li>You may sell within a few years</li>
  <li>You prefer a traditional look or your HOA favors shingles</li>
  <li>Your roof has many valleys, dormers and penetrations that add cost to metal</li>
</ul>
<p>Metal roofing may suit you best if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>You plan to stay in the home for a long time</li>
  <li>Wind performance and a longer expected service life matter most to you</li>
  <li>You like the look of standing seam panels</li>
  <li>You are comfortable with a higher upfront investment</li>
</ul>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Read more about each option on our <a href="/services/asphalt-shingle-roofing/">asphalt shingle roofing</a> and <a href="/services/metal-roofing/">metal roofing</a> pages. If your current roof is aging, our article on <a href="/blog/roof-repair-or-replacement-friendswood/">roof repair versus replacement</a> can help you decide whether it is time. When you are ready, <a href="/contact/">request an estimate</a> and ask us to price both materials so you can compare them for your own home.</p>
